"61_winui_shell_tests.mst — WinUiShellTests, the headless half of WG1's
 acceptance gate (docs/sprints/tests_wg1.md, 'Unit / in-language tests').
 Exercises world/91_winui_shell.mst: WinArena's GC-stability contract, the
 UTF-16 boundary in both directions, WinApi's constants and struct offsets,
 WinShell's theme read and its no-window error.

 WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: anything that opens a window. WG0's Δ 11
 measured that several VMs share one process under `cargo test`, in parallel
 threads, sharing one window-class namespace and one desktop — and a VISIBLE
 window with a message loop is not a thing to create inside a parallel test
 harness. Gate items 1-7 (the window, the pump, the thread invariant, the
 snap, the title round-trip, the clean exit, the DPI arithmetic) live in the
 `macvm-winui` binary's own gate, where there is exactly one process, one
 thread and one window. This file is what remains once that line is drawn,
 and drawing it is a decision, not an omission.

 WHERE THE CLASSES COME FROM. They live in the conditional winui layer
 (world/winui.list), not in world.list, so the base world stays
 byte-identical. This file therefore needs BOTH layer files loaded first,
 and world/tests/tests.list names `../90_winui_probe.mst` and
 `../91_winui_shell.mst` immediately above this line for exactly that
 reason: an unresolved capitalised name inside a method body is a COMPILE
 error in this dialect and the guest has no `Smalltalk at: #WinShell` to
 reach one dynamically, so a test file for a layered world cannot guard
 itself at run time (WG0's Δ 6, inherited rather than re-litigated).

 Runs ONLY on Windows (the platformName guard in 99_run_all.mst), loads
 everywhere — an FFI pragma resolves at CALL time, so on macOS these are
 inert definitions.

 TWO SKIP LEVELS, both announced, never silent:
   * NOT WINDOWS — the runner never calls us.
   * NO DATABASE — windows_api.db is a ~90 MB machine-local artifact, not a
     build input. Every constant, every struct offset and therefore the
     arena itself (VirtualAlloc needs MEM_COMMIT) comes from it by design,
     so without it those skip with a named reason and nothing fails. The one
     test that needs neither it nor Win32 still runs."

TestCase subclass: WinUiShellTests [
    WinUiShellTests class >> isEnabled ^ <Boolean> [
        "WG9-2: the gate that used to live only in
         `99_run_all.mst`'s guards, moved here so DISCOVERY can see
         it too (`TestRunner allTestClasses`). platform-gated; see 99_run_all.mst's bookkeeping note."
        ^Smalltalk platformName == #windows
    ]


    runAll [
        "The no-window error path is pure Smalltalk — WinShell answers it
         before it touches winkb, Win32 or the arena — so it runs whenever
         this file runs at all."
        self runTest: #testShellWithoutWindowFailsCleanly do: [ self testShellWithoutWindowFailsCleanly ].

        WinApi winkbAvailable ifFalse: [
            Transcript showCr:
                '  SKIP WinUiShellTests (5 of 6) — windows_api.db absent on this machine.'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       WG1 resolves every constant and every struct offset from it, and the'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       arena itself needs MEM_COMMIT/PAGE_READWRITE to exist at all, so'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testArenaGivesStableAddresses, testUtf16InArena,'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testApiConstantsResolve, testMsgStructOffsets and'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       testDarkModePreferenceIsReadNotInvented cannot run. The database is'.
            Transcript showCr:
                '       a machine-local artifact, not a build input; nothing here is broken.'.
            ^self ].

        self runTest: #testArenaGivesStableAddresses do: [ self testArenaGivesStableAddresses ].
        self runTest: #testUtf16InArena do: [ self testUtf16InArena ].
        self runTest: #testApiConstantsResolve do: [ self testApiConstantsResolve ].
        self runTest: #testMsgStructOffsets do: [ self testMsgStructOffsets ].
        self runTest: #testDarkModePreferenceIsReadNotInvented
             do: [ self testDarkModePreferenceIsReadNotInvented ].
    ]

    "── WinArena: the rule WG0 remembered, now pinned ────────────────────"

    testArenaGivesStableAddresses [
        "WG0's Δ 5 as an assertion rather than a comment. A direct `Alien
         new:` is GC-managed storage that a scavenge RELOCATES; arena memory
         is VirtualAlloc'd and does not move, and every byte Win32 reads
         lives there. Forcing a scavenge between the two reads is what makes
         this a test of the property rather than a test of arithmetic.

         The Alien is deliberately re-made from the address AFTER the
         scavenge: an Alien wrapping a foreign address is a fixed number in
         an object that may itself move, and re-deriving it proves the
         ADDRESS survived, not that the wrapper did."
        | a b marker |
        a := WinArena alloc: 32.
        b := WinArena alloc: 32.
        self assert: a > 0.
        self deny: a = b.
        "Distinct, non-overlapping and 8-aligned — the bump allocator's whole
         contract, and misalignment would fault a 64-bit struct write on
         some paths and silently work on others."
        self assert: b >= (a + 32).
        self assert: a \\ 8 equals: 0.
        self assert: b \\ 8 equals: 0.

        marker := 16r123456789ABC.
        WinArena i64At: a put: marker.
        WinArena u32At: b put: 4294967295.

        Smalltalk gcScavenge.
        Smalltalk gcScavenge.

        self assert: (WinArena i64At: a) equals: marker.
        self assert: (WinArena u32At: b) equals: 4294967295.
        self assert: (WinArena i32At: b) equals: -1.
        "And a fresh allocation after a scavenge still lands inside the same
         region — i.e. the arena's base itself did not move."
        self assert: (WinArena alloc: 8) > b
    ]

    testUtf16InArena [
        "The boundary, both ways. `asUtf16Alien` is pure conversion into
         movable heap storage that may never cross the FFI; `nativeUtf16:`
         is the copy into arena memory that may, and answers the ADDRESS
         because Alien publishes no address accessor to the guest at all.
         `stringFromUtf16At:units:` is WG1's addition: nothing until now read
         text back OUT of a foreign buffer, and GetWindowTextW does.

         The astral case is included because a window title is a UTF-8
         String and U+1F600 is where a naive UTF-16 encoder produces two
         plausible wrong answers (a lone code unit, or a mojibake pair)."
        | plain addr back astral aaddr |
        plain := 'macVM'.
        addr := WinArena nativeUtf16: plain.
        self assert: addr > 0.
        "Little-endian code units, then the two-byte NUL."
        1 to: 5 do: [ :i |
            self assert: (WinArena u16At: addr + ((i - 1) * 2)) equals: (plain at: i) asInteger ].
        self assert: (WinArena u16At: addr + 10) equals: 0.
        back := WinArena stringFromUtf16At: addr units: 5.
        self assert: back equals: plain.

        "Memoised by value: the same literal must not consume the arena
         twice (WinArena's own comment says why that matters more than
         speed)."
        self assert: (WinArena nativeUtf16: plain) equals: addr.

        "Astral: U+1F600 is four UTF-8 bytes in, one surrogate PAIR across
         the boundary, and the same four bytes back."
        astral := String new: 4.
        astral at: 1 put: 240 asCharacter.
        astral at: 2 put: 159 asCharacter.
        astral at: 3 put: 152 asCharacter.
        astral at: 4 put: 128 asCharacter.
        aaddr := WinArena nativeUtf16: astral.
        self assert: (WinArena u16At: aaddr) equals: 16rD83D.
        self assert: (WinArena u16At: aaddr + 2) equals: 16rDE00.
        self assert: (WinArena stringFromUtf16At: aaddr units: 2) equals: astral.
        self assert: ((WinArena stringFromUtf16At: aaddr units: 2) codePoints at: 1)
             equals: 16r1F600.

        "The scratch block is REUSED by contract — the second call clobbers
         the first, which is why a caller keeping the address across calls
         would be wrong and why titles use it while class names do not."
        self assert: (WinArena scratchUtf16: 'one') equals: (WinArena scratchUtf16: 'two').
        self assert: (WinArena stringFromUtf16At: (WinArena scratchUtf16: 'two') units: 3)
             equals: 'two'
    ]

    "── WinApi: the numbers come from the database (D2) ──────────────────"

    testApiConstantsResolve [
        "Every constant WG1 names, asked for BY NAME. None of these is
         transcribed anywhere in world/91: a hand-copied flag word does not
         fault, it produces a window with the wrong style and no error
         anywhere, which is the failure mode D2 exists to prevent.

         The three signed ones are the point of the second half.
         CW_USEDEFAULT, HKEY_CURRENT_USER and the PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2
         context are all C casts of negative ints, and each arrives with C's
         own signedness — the only form that fits a SmallInt and therefore
         the only form the FFI can marshal. HKEY_CURRENT_USER in particular
         marshals sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFF80000001, which IS the 64-bit
         predefined key value; a 'helpfully' unsigned 0x80000001 would be a
         different key and RegGetValueW would simply fail."
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW') equals: 16rCF0000.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'SW_SHOW') equals: 5.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'DWMWA_USE_IMMERSIVE_DARK_MODE') equals: 20.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'DWMWA_SYSTEMBACKDROP_TYPE') equals: 38.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'DWMSBT_MAINWINDOW') equals: 2.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI') equals: 96.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CLOSE') equals: 16.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'WM_QUIT') equals: 18.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'IDC_ARROW') equals: 32512.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT') equals: -2147483648.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'HKEY_CURRENT_USER') equals: -2147483647.
        self assert: (WinApi constant: 'DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2') equals: -4.

        "An unknown name must NAME itself in the error — every time, not
         once: `constant:` caches hits and must not cache misses, or the
         second ask would answer a stale nil."
        self assert: ((self constantErrorFor: 'NO_SUCH_WG1_CONSTANT')
             includesSubstring: 'NO_SUCH_WG1_CONSTANT').
        self assert: ((self constantErrorFor: 'NO_SUCH_WG1_CONSTANT')
             includesSubstring: 'NO_SUCH_WG1_CONSTANT').

        "HRESULT semantics, which every DWM call in WG1 depends on: failure
         is exactly 'negative once narrowed'. E_INVALIDARG arrives from the
         FFI zero-extended as 2147942487, and both `= 0` and `< 2^31` would
         classify it wrongly."
        self assert: (WinApi hresultOk: 0).
        self deny: (WinApi hresultOk: 2147942487).
        self assert: (WinApi i32: 2147942487) equals: -2147024809
    ]

    constantErrorFor: aName [
        ^[ WinApi constant: aName. '<<no error>>' ]
            on: Error do: [ :e | e messageText ]
    ]

    testMsgStructOffsets [
        "MSG is the struct WG2's door will live inside, so its layout is
         pinned here even though WG1's Rust half owns the only MSG that
         actually exists. No offset is compared against a hardcoded number —
         that would re-transcribe the header into the test and pin nothing.
         What is asserted is that every query ANSWERS, that offsets strictly
         increase in declaration order, and that the recorded SIZE agrees
         with the recorded OFFSETS (two different database tables —
         types.size_bits and struct_fields.byte_offset — so their agreement
         is evidence rather than tautology).

         Δ (WG1, measured): tests_wg1.md asks for `size = last offset + its
         width`, which is the invariant WG0 found for WNDCLASSW. It does not
         hold here and should not: MSG's last member is a POINT at offset 36,
         POINT is 8 bytes, and MSG is 48 — 44 rounded up to the struct's
         8-byte alignment. TAIL PADDING is exactly what `sizeOf:` exists to
         report and what deriving a size from offsets would silently drop, so
         the honest invariant is `lastOffset + width <= size` with the gap
         smaller than the alignment. WNDCLASSW satisfies the stricter form
         only because its last member is a pointer already at a multiple of
         8; that was a coincidence of that struct, not a rule."
        | names offs previous size last |
        names := #('hwnd' 'message' 'wParam' 'lParam' 'time' 'pt').
        offs := OrderedCollection new.
        previous := -1.
        names do: [ :f |
            | o |
            o := WinApi offsetOf: 'MSG' field: f.
            self deny: o isNil.
            self assert: o > previous.
            previous := o.
            offs add: o ].
        size := WinApi sizeOf: 'MSG'.
        last := (offs at: offs size) + (WinApi sizeOf: 'POINT').
        self assert: last <= size.
        self assert: size - last < 8.
        self assert: size \\ 8 equals: 0.
        "RECT is the struct WinShell actually reads, and it has no padding to
         forgive: four LONGs, 16 bytes, exactly."
        self assert: (WinApi sizeOf: 'RECT') equals: 16.
        self assert: (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'left') equals: 0.
        self assert: ((WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'bottom')
                        + 4) equals: (WinApi sizeOf: 'RECT')
    ]

    "── WinShell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    testDarkModePreferenceIsReadNotInvented [
        "D3: the dark-mode value comes from the SYSTEM, not from a preference
         this project invents. So the only honest assertions are about the
         SHAPE of the answer — a REG_DWORD that is 0 or 1, or nil when the
         value is not on this machine — and about the default when the read
         fails being light. Asserting a particular value would be asserting
         which theme the developer happened to be running.

         The registry read is also the only thing in this layer that touches
         a machine's configuration, and it must not raise when the key is
         missing: `dwordAt:path:value:` answers nil, because a missing
         preference is a normal state of the world."
        | v absent |
        v := WinShell appsUseLightTheme.
        v isNil ifFalse: [
            self assert: (v = 0 or: [ v = 1 ]).
            self assert: WinShell systemPrefersDark equals: v = 0 ].
        "Whatever the machine says, the answer is a Boolean and it agrees
         with the brush the class would register."
        self assert: (WinShell systemPrefersDark == true
                        or: [ WinShell systemPrefersDark == false ]).
        self assert: WinShell backgroundColorRef
             equals: (WinShell systemPrefersDark ifTrue: [ 16r202020 ] ifFalse: [ 16rF3F3F3 ]).

        "A value that certainly is not there answers nil rather than
         raising, which is what makes 'light is the honest default' a
         reachable path and not a comment."
        absent := WinApi
            dwordAt: (WinApi constant: 'HKEY_CURRENT_USER')
            path: 'Software\Macvm\NoSuchKeyForWG1'
            value: 'NoSuchValue'.
        self assert: absent isNil
    ]

    testShellWithoutWindowFailsCleanly [
        "Every window verb must answer a clean guest error NAMING itself
         when there is no window — never a Win32 call on a null HWND.
         SetWindowTextW(NULL, ...) was measured to answer TRUE and change
         nothing, which is the worst of both worlds: no window, no error,
         and a caller convinced it worked.

         This test creates nothing and needs no database, which is why it is
         above the winkb guard: `ensureOpen:` answers before anything below
         it is consulted."
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell setTitle: 'nope' ])
             includesSubstring: 'setTitle:').
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell title ]) includesSubstring: 'title').
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell clientRect ]) includesSubstring: 'clientRect').
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell close ]) includesSubstring: 'close').
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell hwnd ]) includesSubstring: 'hwnd').
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell windowThreadId ])
             includesSubstring: 'windowThreadId').
        "The message says what to do about it, because an error a user cannot
         act on is a stack trace with better manners."
        self assert: ((self shellErrorFor: [ WinShell close ]) includesSubstring: 'openMain').
        "And the accessors that CAN answer without a window do, rather than
         raising: a host asking 'is there a window yet' must not have to
         catch an exception to find out."
        self deny: WinShell isOpen.
        self assert: WinShell hwndValue equals: 0
    ]

    shellErrorFor: aBlock [
        ^[ aBlock value. '<<no error>>' ] on: Error do: [ :e | e messageText ]
    ]
]
